**Reused evidence**
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0002"],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.3","5B.7","5B.10"]}

**New evidence**
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Philippines' Marcos signals continuity ahead with new economic team","publisher":"Reuters via Investing.com","publication_date":"2022-05-25","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy/philippines-marcos-names-new-finance-chief-and-central-bank-governor-2830330","claim":"Marcos said the first priority would be the economy and named Benjamin Diokno as finance minister and Felipe Medalla as BSP governor, framing the team as a continuity move to tackle inflation and joblessness.","locator":"lead paragraphs; appointment and first-priority paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting on a contemporaneous announcement is strong for the appointment and stated policy intent, though it does not by itself prove later economic effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 target period and tied to the new administration's initial economic setup.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's appointment authority and stated economic priorities.","contrary_evidence":["Continuity with prior economic policy may limit evidence of new structural reform.","Inflation and debt pressures were inherited, so the announcement alone does not prove improved stewardship."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Anti-trust chief to lead Philippines' economic planning agency","publisher":"Euronews via Reuters","publication_date":"2022-05-24","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/05/24/us-philippines-marcos","claim":"Arsenio Balisacan accepted Marcos's offer to become economic planning chief; Reuters described him as an economist with prior experience leading the same planning agency and said Marcos was not selecting on political affiliation.","locator":"article body paragraphs identifying Balisacan and the selection criteria","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters-sourced reporting provides a reliable contemporaneous account of the appointment and the stated nonpartisan selection rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-period appointment decision made before Marcos took office in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's cabinet selection, with the caveat that the outlet reports Marcos's stated nonpolitical criterion rather than independently proving it.","contrary_evidence":["The selection rationale is self-reported by the incoming administration.","Competence claims are partly inferential from resume and prior service, not direct outcome evidence."],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.5","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"State of the Nation Address of His Excellency Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr. President of the Philippines to the Congress of the Philippines","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/presidential-speech/state-of-the-nation-address-of-his-excellency-ferdinand-r-marcos-jr-president-of-the-philippines-to-the-congress-of-the-philippines/","claim":"Marcos promised sound fiscal management, tax administration reforms, improved spending efficiency, productivity-enhancing investments, support for ecozones and digital taxation, and customs anti-misinvoicing measures.","locator":"economy section beginning 'In terms of the economy' and subsequent paragraphs on tax/customs/investment policy","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"This is the primary presidential speech text, so it is strong evidence of stated policy intent and announced priorities, though not of implementation by itself.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct target-year speech delivered one month into the Marcos presidency.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos as his own stated program within presidential authority.","contrary_evidence":["Announcements do not prove execution.","Inflation was already elevated when the speech was delivered, so the speech is partly reactive to inherited conditions."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"State of the Nation Address of His Excellency Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr. President of the Philippines to the Congress of the Philippines","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/presidential-speech/state-of-the-nation-address-of-his-excellency-ferdinand-r-marcos-jr-president-of-the-philippines-to-the-congress-of-the-philippines/","claim":"Marcos set out rightsizing, budget modernization, e-government, e-commerce, tax reform, and infrastructure-related priority measures, framing them as administrative and regulatory reforms to improve efficiency and competitiveness.","locator":"priority-measures portion of the speech listing the National Government Rightsizing Program, Budget Modernization Bill, E-Government Act, Internet Transaction Act, PIFITA, and BOT/EPIRA amendments","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary presidential text is strong for policy agenda and institutional direction, but not for ultimate implementation effects.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct target-year legislative agenda announced in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos's own agenda-setting within his constitutional and legislative initiative powers.","contrary_evidence":["These are proposed or advocated measures, not completed reforms.","Some items require congressional action and later implementation."],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.5","5B.6","5B.7"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"State of the Nation Address of His Excellency Ferdinand R. Marcos Jr. President of the Philippines to the Congress of the Philippines","publisher":"Presidential Communications Office","publication_date":"2022-07-25","url":"https://pco.gov.ph/presidential-speech/state-of-the-nation-address-of-his-excellency-ferdinand-r-marcos-jr-president-of-the-philippines-to-the-congress-of-the-philippines/","claim":"Marcos linked his medium-term fiscal strategy to growth, poverty, debt, and income targets, and said his administration would keep disbursements high while aiming for macro-fiscal stability and fiscal sustainability.","locator":"medium-term fiscal strategy paragraphs and headline goals list","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary speech text is strong evidence of announced macro goals and fiscal stance, though the targets themselves remain aspirational.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year policy roadmap announced in July 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos as president setting fiscal priorities and targets.","contrary_evidence":["Targets are aspirational and not proof of delivery.","The fiscal roadmap was set amid inherited post-pandemic and global price shocks."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Executive Order No. 10","publisher":"LawPhil","publication_date":"2022-12-29","url":"https://lawphil.net/executive/execord/eo2022/eo_10_2022.html","claim":"Marcos extended reduced import duties on rice, corn, coal, and pork to maintain affordable prices, ensure food security, augment supply, and reduce electricity costs.","locator":"whereas clauses and Section 1 of EO No. 10","source_type":"legal","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The official executive order is definitive evidence of the policy choice and its stated rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Issued in the target year and effective from December 29, 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos as an executive order issued under presidential power, with NEDA-board recommendation noted in the text.","contrary_evidence":["Tariff relief can help consumers but may also reinforce import dependence.","The measure addresses price pressure, not structural productivity by itself."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Executive Board Concludes 2022 Article IV Consultation with the Philippines","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-28","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/28/pr22409-imf-executive-board-concludes-2022-article-iv-consultation-with-the-philippines","claim":"The IMF said the economy remained fundamentally sound but that Marcos's new administration faced the tasks of calibrating policy to protect price stability, building fiscal buffers, and raising long-term growth; inflation and the external position had weakened in 2022.","locator":"press-release summary and Executive Board Assessment","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The IMF is a strong independent macroeconomic source and the press release directly addresses the 2022 policy environment.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year review of the Marcos administration's first months and 2022 macro conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable: the policy mix and recovery strategy are Marcos-administration choices, while the inflation shock and external headwinds are partly inherited or external.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF still described the economy as fundamentally sound, which limits any claim of severe mismanagement.","Much of the pressure stemmed from global commodity and tightening-financial-conditions shocks."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"GDP Expands by 7.2 Percent in the Fourth Quarter of 2022, and by 7.6 Percent in Full-year 2022","publisher":"Philippine Statistics Authority","publication_date":"2023-01-26","url":"https://psa.gov.ph/content/gdp-expands-72-percent-fourth-quarter-2022-and-76-percent-full-year-2022?vcode=Ekt7j","claim":"The Philippine economy grew by 7.6 percent in full-year 2022, with services, manufacturing, and construction as the main contributors, while agriculture contracted slightly in the fourth quarter.","locator":"release summary and industry-growth paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official national accounts data is the authoritative source for the 2022 growth outcome.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"This is the official full-year 2022 output, published immediately after the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Mixed attribution: the growth result reflects Marcos-era policy and reopening, but also inherited pandemic rebound dynamics and external conditions.","contrary_evidence":["Strong growth partly reflects reopening and base effects rather than uniquely Marcos-driven structural change.","Agriculture was still weak, limiting any claim of evenly shared productivity gains."],"lenses":["5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Summary Inflation Report Consumer Price Index (2018=100): December 2022","publisher":"Philippine Statistics Authority","publication_date":"2023-01-05","url":"https://psa.gov.ph/content/summary-inflation-report-consumer-price-index-2018100-december-2022","claim":"The Philippines' average inflation for 2022 was 5.8 percent, and December 2022 inflation reached 8.1 percent, with food and non-alcoholic beverages among the main drivers.","locator":"release summary and Table A / year-to-date inflation paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official price statistics are authoritative for the 2022 inflation outcome.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct full-year 2022 outcome published at the start of 2023.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable: the inflation spike was a challenge for the Marcos administration's first months, but it was also driven by global commodities, supply shocks, and inherited post-pandemic dynamics.","contrary_evidence":["High inflation is negative evidence against stewardship and household welfare.","The release does not by itself identify policy responsibility beyond the macro outcome."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Unemployment rate reached 5.4 percent","publisher":"Philippine Statistics Authority","publication_date":"2023-02","url":"https://psa.gov.ph/statistics/labor-force-survey/press-release/node/1684059859","claim":"The PSA reported that the Philippines' 2022 unemployment rate was 5.4 percent, with notable regional variation and no provinces reaching double-digit unemployment in the annual distribution.","locator":"annual unemployment release text and regional distribution paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official labor-force data is the authoritative source for the 2022 employment outcome.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year annual labor-market result published shortly after the year closed.","ruler_attribution":"Mixed attribution: the labor-market recovery is partly linked to Marcos-era reopening and policy continuity, but it also reflects broader post-pandemic normalization.","contrary_evidence":["The labor market remained uneven by region and underemployment was still significant.","The statistic alone does not isolate Marcos's causal contribution."],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PH debt-to-GDP ratio down to 60.9% in 2022, consistent with medium-term fiscal plan","publisher":"Department of Finance","publication_date":"2023-02-02","url":"https://www.dof.gov.ph/ph-debt-to-gdp-ratio-down-to-60-9-in-2022-consistent-with-medium-term-fiscal-plan/","claim":"The DOF said the Philippines' debt-to-GDP ratio fell to 60.9 percent at end-2022, below the 61.8 percent target in the medium-term fiscal framework, and tied the result to the fiscal plan and strong GDP growth.","locator":"lead paragraphs and fiscal-plan paragraphs","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official fiscal reporting is strong for the debt ratio and the stated fiscal framework, though the causal attribution to policy is partly interpretive.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"End-of-2022 fiscal outcome, reported immediately afterward.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable: the fiscal framework and spending choices are Marcos-administration choices, but debt ratios also move with growth, exchange rates, and inherited pandemic borrowing.","contrary_evidence":["Debt remained high relative to the pre-pandemic period.","The positive ratio change does not by itself prove superior spending quality or distribution."],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"PHILIPPINES: Reducing Inequality Key to Becoming a Middle-Class Society Free of Poverty","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-11-24","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/11/24/ph-reducing-inequality-key-to-becoming-a-middle-class-society-free-of-poverty","claim":"The World Bank said policies supporting employment, education, rural development, and social protection can reduce inequality; it also noted that inequality remained high and that rising food prices hurt vulnerable households.","locator":"press-release body paragraphs on inequality, poverty, and food-price effects","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"World Bank analysis is a strong independent source for baseline inequality and the distributional context facing Marcos in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Target-year distributional context published in 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly context and inherited baseline: the report describes long-run structural inequality that predated Marcos, while still illuminating the welfare constraints he inherited.","contrary_evidence":["The report's progress in poverty reduction predates the Marcos presidency, limiting direct ruler credit.","It is a structural baseline report, not a direct evaluation of Marcos's 2022 execution."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Agricultural reforms key to strong PH economy - Marcos","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-11","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1205432","claim":"Marcos said agricultural policy should prioritize both production and farmers' incomes, and the article describes Kadiwa ng Pangulo pop-up stores and aid distribution to farmers and indigenous beneficiaries as part of the administration's food-security push.","locator":"report paragraphs on farmers' income, Kadiwa stores, and aid distribution in Butuan","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"PNA is an official government news outlet and the claim concerns documented program activity, but the economic effectiveness remains only partly observable in the article.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Target-year agricultural and food-security activity within 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to Marcos because the article reports his own policy framing and administration-led program activity.","contrary_evidence":["This is still mostly program description and rhetoric, not audited effect on productivity or inequality.","Kadiwa and aid distribution are helpful but not macro-transformational by themselves."],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Kadiwa stores serve 1.22 million households, 450 farmer groups","publisher":"Philippine News Agency","publication_date":"2022-12-31","url":"https://www.pna.gov.ph/articles/1191773","claim":"By the end of 2022, Kadiwa stores had reportedly served 1.22 million households and 450 farmer groups with lower-priced food products.","locator":"lead paragraph and captioned summary of Malacañang data","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"The scale claim is official and concrete, but it is still a program output rather than an independently audited welfare effect.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the target year and tied to Marcos's anti-inflation food-access effort.","ruler_attribution":"Directly attributable to the Marcos administration's food-price and farm-market interventions.","contrary_evidence":["Scale is meaningful but still modest relative to the national economy.","The article does not show longer-run productivity or poverty impact."],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I screened roughly 30-plus documents across PCO, Reuters/Reuters-syndicated reports, IMF, PSA, DOF, World Bank, LawPhil, and PNA.
- I kept 12 distinct URLs with non-duplicative claims; the retained set centers on the 2022 cabinet/economic team, Marcos's SONA fiscal and regulatory agenda, tariff relief, IMF macro assessment, PSA macro outputs, DOF debt reporting, and food-security distribution programs.
- I rejected or de-emphasized duplicate mirrors of the same SONA, repeated Reuters syndications of the same appointment story, and later follow-up pieces from 2023-2026 because they did not add target-year evidence.
- I dropped partisan or advocacy-adjacent items that mostly restated praise without new evidence, including Senate commendations and PSE praise for the economic team.
- I excluded campaign-period promise pieces from March-May 2022 unless they were later confirmed by 2022 administration actions.
- I excluded later tariff and anti-hunger follow-ups from 2023-2026 because they were outside the target year.
- I did not re-fetch structured local indicators already provided in the prior; they were used as context, not as standalone evidence citations.

**Remaining gaps**
- Direct, independent evidence on anti-corruption and capture in 2022 is still thin. I have policy-intent evidence on rightsizing, customs streamlining, and budget modernization, but not much audited implementation on favoritism or oligarchic privilege.
- Household-level distribution data for 2022 is limited. I have strong macro data and some food-access program outputs, but not a clean micro distribution picture for the Marcos period itself.
- More search would help on 2022 execution of the SONA legislative agenda, COA/audit findings, and whether customs/tax reforms were actually implemented or just announced.
- More search would also help distinguish how much of the 2022 growth and labor-market improvement was Marcos-era policy versus inherited reopening and rebound effects.
